      "message": "I had a theory on how this could break, and was able to confirm it locally - this moves all the side-effect expressions *before* the indexed value. This can alter the outcome, if the indexed value depends on the side effects. For example, if you change line 26 of ArrayFolding.sksl to be:\n\n    int noFlatten0 \u003d (int[3](globalValue, side_effecting(2), 3))[0];\n\n... the generated GLSL will still call side_effecting() before evaluating globalValue:\n\n    int _10_noFlatten0 \u003d (side_effecting_ii(2) , globalValue);\n\nThis is obviously really esoteric and unlikely, but it does seem like we should try to avoid this kind of reordering. The GLSL spec doesn\u0027t explicitly mention anything like a sequence point in array construction, it just says \"The\narguments are assigned in order, starting at element 0, to the elements of the constructed array\".",
